#import "@local/fgbooks:0.14.0": speech, stanza

== THE SIXTH STAGE

Now I saw that they went on to the rise a little way off, which had been raised up as a lookout for pilgrims. That was the place from which Christian had his first sight of Faithful his brother. So here they sat down and rested. Here they also ate and drank and made merry, because they had been delivered from so dangerous an enemy. As they sat eating, Christiana asked the guide whether he had been hurt at all in the battle. Then Mr. Great-Heart said, No, only a little in my flesh; yet even that will be so far from doing me harm that it is a proof, here and now, of my love to my master and to you, and will be a means, by grace, to increase my reward at last.

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: But were you not afraid, good sir, when you saw him coming with his club?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: It is my duty, he said, to distrust my own ability, so that I may rely on Him who is stronger than all.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: But what did you think when he knocked you to the ground at the first blow?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Why, I thought, he said, that my Master himself was treated the same way, and yet it was he who conquered in the end. 2 Cor 4:10, 11; Rom 8:37.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Matthew]: Whatever the rest of you may think, I think God has been wonderfully good to us, both in bringing us out of this valley and in delivering us out of the hand of this enemy. For my part, I see no reason why we should distrust our God any more, since he has now, and in such a place as this, given us such proof of his love. Then they got up and went on.]

Now a little ahead of them stood an oak; and under it, when they reached it, they found an old pilgrim fast asleep. They knew that he was a pilgrim by his clothes, his staff, and his belt.

So the guide, Mr. Great-Heart, woke him; and the old gentleman, as he looked up, cried out, What’s the matter? Who are you, and what is your business here?

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Come, man, do not get so heated; there is no one here but friends. Yet the old man gets up, and stands on his guard, and demands to know who they are. Then the guide said, My name is Great-Heart: I am the guide of these pilgrims who are going to the Celestial country.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: Then Mr. Honest said, I beg your pardon: I was afraid you were some of that company who robbed Little-Faith of his money a while ago; but now that I look about me more carefully, I see you are more honest people.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Why, what would or could you have done to help yourself, if we really had been some of that company?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: Done! Why, I would have fought as long as there was breath in me; and if I had done so, I am sure you could never have got the better of me; for a Christian can never be overcome unless he gives in of his own accord.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Well said, father Honest, the guide answered; for by this I know you are a cock of the right kind, for you have spoken the truth.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: And by this I also know that you know what true pilgrimage is; for everyone else thinks that we are the easiest of all to overcome.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Well, now that we have met so happily, please let me ask your name, and the name of the place you came from.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: I cannot tell you my name, but I came from the town of Stupidity: it lies about four degrees beyond the city of Destruction.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Oh, are you from that country? Then I think I can half guess who you are: your name is Old Honesty, is it not?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: So the old gentleman blushed and said, Not honesty in the abstract, but Honest is my name; and I hope that my nature may agree with what I am called. But, sir, the old gentleman said, how could you guess that I am such a man, since I came from such a place?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: I had heard of you before from my Master; for he knows all things that are done on the earth. But I have often wondered that anyone should come from your place; for your town is worse than the city of Destruction itself.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: Yes, we lie farther from the sun, and so we are colder and more numb. But if a man were in a mountain of ice, yet if the Sun of righteousness arises on him, his frozen heart will feel a thaw; and so it has been with me.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: I believe it, father Honest, I believe it; for I know it is true.]

Then the old gentleman greeted all the pilgrims with a holy kiss of love, and asked them their names, and how they had fared since they set out on their pilgrimage.

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: Then Christiana said, I suppose you have heard my name; good Christian was my husband, and these four are his children. But can you imagine how the old gentleman was moved when she told him who she was? He skipped, he smiled, he blessed them with a thousand good wishes, saying,]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: I have heard much of your husband, and of the travels and wars he went through in his day. Let it be said for your comfort, the name of your husband rings through all these parts of the world: his faith, his courage, his endurance, and his sincerity through it all had made his name famous. Then he turned to the boys and asked them their names, which they told him. Then he said to them, Matthew, be like Matthew the tax collector, not in vice, but in virtue. Matt 10:3. Samuel, he said, be like Samuel the prophet, a man of faith and prayer. Ps 99:6. Joseph, he said, be like Joseph in Potiphar’s house, chaste, and one who flees from temptation. Gen 39. And James, be like James the just, and like James the brother of our Lord. Acts 1:13. Then they told him about Mercy, and how she had left her town and her relatives to come along with Christiana and her sons. At that the honest old man said, Mercy is your name: by mercy you shall be sustained and carried through all the difficulties that will attack you on your way, till you come to the place where you shall look the Fountain of mercy in the face with comfort. All this while the guide, Mr. Great-Heart, was very well pleased, and smiled on his companions.]

Now, as they walked along together, the guide asked the old gentleman whether he knew a Mr. Fearing, who came on pilgrimage from his part of the country.

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: Yes, very well, he said. He was a man who had the root of the matter in him; but he was one of the most troublesome pilgrims I ever met in all my days.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: I see you knew him, for you have given a very true description of him.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: Knew him! I was a close companion of his; I was with him almost all the time; when he first began to think about what would come upon us hereafter, I was with him.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: I was his guide from my Master’s house to the gates of the Celestial City.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: Then you found him a troublesome one.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: I did; but I could bear it very well; for men of my calling are often entrusted with the charge of men like him.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: Well then, please let us hear a little about him, and how he conducted himself under your guidance.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Why, he was always afraid that he would fall short of the place he wanted to reach. Everything he heard anyone speak of frightened him, if it had even the least appearance of opposition in it. I heard that he lay roaring at the Slough of Despond for more than a month on end; nor did he dare venture across, though he saw several go over before him, and many of them offered to lend him a hand. He would not go back again, either. The Celestial City—he said he would die if he did not come to it; and yet he was downcast at every difficulty, and stumbled at every straw that anyone threw in his way. Well, after he had lain at the Slough of Despond a great while, as I have told you, one sunny morning, I do not know how, he ventured, and so got over; but when he was over, he could hardly believe it. He had, I think, a Slough of Despond in his mind, a slough that he carried everywhere with him, or else he could never have been as he was. So he came up to the gate, you know what I mean, that stands at the head of this way, and there too he stood a good while before he would venture to knock. When the gate was opened, he would step back and make way for others, and say that he was not worthy. For though he got to the gate before some, yet many of them went in before him. There the poor man would stand shaking and shrinking; I dare say it would have moved anyone’s heart to pity to see him. Yet he would not go back again. At last he took the knocker that hung on the gate in his hand, and gave a small rap or two; then someone opened to him, but he shrank back as before. The one who opened stepped out after him and said, You trembling one, what do you want? At that he fell down to the ground. The one who spoke to him marveled to see him so faint, so he said to him, Peace be to you; get up, for I have set the door open for you; come in, for you are blessed. With that he got up and went in trembling; and when he was in, he was ashamed to show his face. Well, after he had been received there a while, as you know the custom is, he was told to go on his way, and was also told the way he should take. So he went on till he came to our house; but as he behaved at the gate, so he did at my Master the Interpreter’s door. He lay about there in the cold a good while before he would venture to call; yet he would not go back: and the nights were long and cold then. In fact, he had a note of necessity in his bosom to my master, asking him to receive him and grant him the comfort of his house, and also to allow him a strong and valiant conductor, because he was himself such a chicken-hearted man; and yet for all that he was afraid to call at the door. So he hung about, up and down near there, till, poor man, he had almost perished with cold; indeed, so great was his dejection that, though he saw several others get in by knocking, he was still afraid to venture. At last, I think, I looked out of the window, and seeing a man going up and down about the door, I went out to him and asked who he was; but, poor man, the water stood in his eyes; so I saw what he needed. So I went in and told it in the house, and we made it known to our Lord: so he sent me out again to beg him to come in; but I dare say I had hard work to do it. At last he came in; and I will say this for my Lord, he was wonderfully loving to him. There were only a few good bits on the table, but some of them were laid on his plate. Then he presented the note; and my Lord looked at it and said his request should be granted. So when he had been there a good while, he seemed to take some heart, and to be a little more comforted. For my Master, you must know, is one of a very tender heart, especially to those who are afraid; so he behaved toward him in whatever way would most encourage him. Well, when he had seen the things of the place, and was ready to set out on his journey to the city, my Lord, as he did for Christian before, gave him a bottle of spirits and some comforting things to eat. So we set out, and I went ahead of him; but the man was a man of few words, only he would sigh aloud.]

When we came to where the three fellows were hanged, he said that he feared that would be his end too. Only he seemed glad when he saw the cross and the sepulcher. There, I confess, he wanted to stay a little to look; and for a while afterward he seemed a little more cheerful. When he came to the Hill Difficulty, he did not balk at that, nor did he much fear the lions: for you must know that his troubles were not about things like these; his fear was about being accepted at last.

I got him into the house Beautiful, I think, before he was willing. Also, when he was in, I introduced him to the young women of the place; but he was ashamed to put himself forward much in company. He wanted very much to be alone; yet he always loved good talk, and would often get behind the screen to hear it. He also loved very much to see ancient things, and to ponder them in his mind. He told me afterward that he loved being in those two houses he had last come from, that is, at the gate and at the Interpreter’s, but that he did not dare be so bold as to ask.

When we went on from the house Beautiful, down the hill into the Valley of Humiliation, he went down as well as I ever saw a man go in my life; for he did not care how lowly he was, so long as he might be happy at last. Indeed, I think there was a kind of sympathy between that Valley and him; for I never saw him better in all his pilgrimage than he was in that Valley.

Here he would lie down, embrace the ground, and kiss the very flowers that grew in this valley. Lam 3:27–29. He would now be up every morning at daybreak, pacing and walking back and forth in the valley.

But when he came to the entrance of the Valley of the Shadow of Death, I thought I would lose my man: not that he had any inclination to go back—that he always abhorred—but he was ready to die of fear. Oh, the hobgoblins will get me! the hobgoblins will get me! he cried; and I could not talk him out of it. He made such a noise and such an outcry here that, had they only heard him, it would have been enough to encourage them to come and fall on us.

But I took very careful note of this: this valley was as quiet when we went through it as I ever knew it before or since. I suppose those enemies here had been given a special restraint by our Lord just then, and a command not to meddle until Mr. Fearing had passed through.

It would be too tedious to tell you everything; so we will mention only a passage or two more. When he came to Vanity Fair, I thought he would have fought with all the men in the fair. I was afraid we would both be knocked on the head there, so hot was he against their follies. On the Enchanted Ground he was very wakeful. But when he came to the river where there was no bridge, he was again in great distress. Now, now, he said, he would be drowned forever, and so never see with comfort that face he had come so many miles to behold.

And here also I noticed something very remarkable: the water of that river was lower at this time than I ever saw it in all my life; so at last he went over hardly wetting more than his feet. When he was going up to the gate, I began to take leave of him and to wish him a good welcome above. So he said, I shall, I shall. Then we parted, and I saw him no more.

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: Then it seems all was well with him at last?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Yes, yes, I never had any doubt about him. He was a man of a fine spirit, only he was always kept very low, and that made his life so burdensome to himself and so troublesome to others. Ps 88. He was, more than many, tender about sin: he was so afraid of wronging others that he would often deny himself what was lawful, because he would not cause others to stumble. Rom 14:21; 1 Cor 8:13.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: But what could be the reason that such a good man should spend all his days so much in the dark?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: There are two sorts of reasons for it. One is, the wise God will have it so: some must pipe, and some must weep. Matt 11:16. Now Mr. Fearing was one who played the bass. He and his fellows sound the sackbut, whose notes are more mournful than the notes of other music: though indeed, some say, the bass is the foundation of music. And for my part, I care nothing at all for a profession of religion that does not begin in heaviness of mind. The first string the musician usually touches is the bass, when he means to put everything in tune. God also plays on this string first, when he sets the soul in tune for himself. Only here was Mr. Fearing’s imperfection: he could play no other music but this until near the end of his life.]

\[I venture to talk metaphorically like this to ripen the wits of young readers, and because, in the book of Revelation, the saved are compared to a company of musicians who play on their trumpets and harps and sing their songs before the throne. Rev 5:8; 14:2, 3.\]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: He was a very zealous man, as one can see from the account you have given of him. Difficulties, lions, or Vanity Fair—he did not fear them at all; it was only sin, death, and hell that were a terror to him, because he had some doubts about his share in that celestial country.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: You are right; those were the things that troubled him; and they, as you have well observed, arose from the weakness of his mind on that point, not from weakness of spirit in the practical part of a pilgrim’s life. I truly believe that, as the proverb goes, he could have bitten a firebrand had it stood in his way; but the things that weighed him down, no man has ever yet been able to shake off with ease.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: Then Christiana said, This account of Mr. Fearing has done me good; I thought nobody had ever been like me. But I see there was some likeness between this good man and me; only we differed in two things. His troubles were so great that they broke out; but mine I kept within. His also lay so heavy on him that they kept him from knocking at the houses provided for hospitality; but my trouble was always such as made me knock the louder.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mercy]: If I may also speak my heart, I must say that something of him has also dwelt in me. For I have always been more afraid of the lake, and of losing a place in paradise, than I have been of losing other things. Oh, I thought, if only I may have the happiness of a dwelling there! It is enough, though I part with all the world to win it.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Matthew]: Then Matthew said, Fear was one thing that made me think I was far from having within me that which accompanies salvation. But if it was so with such a good man as he, why should it not also go well with me?]

#speech[#smallcaps[James]: No fears, no grace, James said. Though there is not always grace where there is the fear of hell, yet, to be sure, there is no grace where there is no fear of God.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Well said, James; you have hit the mark. For the fear of God is the beginning of wisdom; and to be sure, those who lack the beginning have neither middle nor end. But we will close our talk about Mr. Fearing here, after we have sent this farewell after him.]

#quote[“Well, Master Fearing, thou didst fear \
Thy God, and wast afraid \
Of doing any thing, while here, \
That would have thee betrayed. \
And didst thou fear the lake and pit? \
Would others do so too! \
For, as for them that want thy wit, \
They do themselves undo.”]

Now I saw that they still went on talking. For after Mr. Great-Heart had finished with Mr. Fearing, Mr. Honest began to tell them about another, whose name was Mr. Self-will. He made himself out to be a pilgrim, Mr. Honest said; but I am sure he never came in at the gate that stands at the head of the way.

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Did you ever talk with him about it?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: Yes, more than once or twice; but he would always be like himself: self-willed. He cared nothing for man, or reasoning, or even example; whatever his mind prompted him to do, that he would do, and nothing else could anyone get him to do.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Tell me, what principles did he hold? For I suppose you can say.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: He held that a man might follow the vices as well as the virtues of pilgrims; and that if he did both, he would certainly be saved.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: What? If he had said that it is possible for the best to be guilty of the vices, as well as to share in the virtues, of pilgrims, he could not have been much blamed; for indeed we are not absolutely exempt from any vice, but only on condition that we watch and strive. But this, I see, is not the point; if I understand you right, you mean that he was of the opinion that it was allowable to be so.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: Yes, yes, that is what I mean, and so he believed and practiced.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: But what grounds did he have for saying so?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: Why, he said he had the Scripture as his warrant.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Please, Mr. Honest, give us a few examples.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: That I will. He said that having to do with other men’s wives had been practiced by David, God’s beloved; and therefore he could do it. He said that having more women than one was a thing that Solomon practiced, and therefore he could do it. He said that Sarah and the godly midwives of Egypt lied, and so did Rahab, who was saved, and therefore he could do it. He said that the disciples went at their Master’s bidding and took away the owner’s donkey, and therefore he could do so too. He said that Jacob got his father’s inheritance by guile and deceit, and therefore he could do so too.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Vile indeed! And are you sure he held this opinion?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: I heard him plead for it, bring Scripture for it, bring arguments for it, and so on.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: An opinion not fit to be allowed anywhere in the world!]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: You must understand me rightly: he did not say that any man might do this; but that those who had the virtues of those who did such things might also do the same.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: But what could be more false than such a conclusion? For this is as much as to say that, because good men in the past have sinned out of weakness, therefore he had leave to do it out of a presumptuous mind; or that, because a child, blown by the wind, or because it stumbled on a stone, fell down and dirtied itself in the mire, therefore he might willfully lie down and wallow in it like a boar. Who could have thought that anyone could be so blinded by the power of lust? But what is written must be true: they “stumble at the word, being disobedient—and to this they were appointed.” 1 Pet 2:8. His supposing that those who give themselves up to the vices of godly men may have the godly men’s virtues is also a delusion as strong as the other. To eat up the sin of God’s people, Hos 4:8, as a dog licks up filth, is no sign that one possesses their virtues. Nor can I believe that one who holds this opinion can at present have faith or love in him. But I know you have made strong objections to him; tell me, what can he say for himself?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: Why, he says that to do this and hold it as one’s opinion seems far more honest than to do it and yet hold the opposite opinion.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: A very wicked answer. For though to give our lusts free rein while our opinions are against such things is bad, yet to sin and plead that we are allowed to do so is worse: the one makes onlookers stumble by accident, the other leads them into the snare.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: There are many of this man’s mind who do not have this man’s mouth; and that is why going on pilgrimage is held in such low esteem.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: You have spoken the truth, and it is to be lamented: but he who fears the King of paradise shall come out of them all.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: There are strange opinions in the world. I know of one who said it was time enough to repent when we come to die.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Such people are not very wise; if that man had had a week to run twenty miles for his life, he would have been unwilling to put off his journey to the last hour of that week.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: You are right; and yet most of those who count themselves pilgrims really do just this. I am, as you see, an old man, and have been a traveler on this road many a day; and I have noticed many things.]

I have seen some who set out as if they would drive the whole world before them, who yet in a few days died as those in the wilderness did, and so never got sight of the promised land. I have seen some who promised nothing when they first set out to be pilgrims, and who one would have thought could not live a day, who have yet proved very good pilgrims. I have seen some who ran hastily forward, who again, after a little while, ran just as fast back again. I have seen some who spoke very well of a pilgrim’s life at first, who after a while spoke just as much against it. I have heard some, when they first set out for paradise, say positively that there is such a place, who, when they were almost there, came back again and said there is none. I have heard some boast what they would do if they were opposed, who, even at a false alarm, have run from faith, the pilgrim’s way, and all.

Now, as they were going on their way like this, someone came running to meet them and said, Gentlemen, and you of the weaker sort, if you love life, save yourselves, for the robbers are ahead of you.

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Then Mr. Great-Heart said, They are the three who attacked Little-Faith some time ago. Well, he said, we are ready for them: so they went on their way. Now at every turn they expected to meet the villains; but whether they had heard of Mr. Great-Heart, or whether they had some other prey in view, they did not come up to the pilgrims.]

Christiana then wished for an inn where she and her children could refresh themselves, because they were weary. Then Mr. Honest said, There is one a little way ahead, where a very honorable disciple named Gaius lives. Rom 16:23. So they all decided to turn in there; and all the more because the old gentleman gave him such a good report. When they came to the door they went in without knocking, for folks do not usually knock at the door of an inn. Then they called for the master of the house, and he came to them. So they asked if they might stay there that night.

#speech[#smallcaps[Gaius]: Yes, gentlemen, if you are true men; for my house is for no one but pilgrims. Then Christiana, Mercy, and the boys were all the more glad, because the innkeeper was a lover of pilgrims. So they asked for rooms, and he showed them one for Christiana and her children and Mercy, and another for Mr. Great-Heart and the old gentleman.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Then Mr. Great-Heart said, Good Gaius, what do you have for supper? For these pilgrims have come far today and are weary.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Gaius]: It is late, Gaius said, so we cannot conveniently go out to look for food; but you shall be welcome to such as we have, if that will satisfy you.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: We will be content with what you have in the house; for since I have tested you, I know you are never without what is fitting.]

Then he went down and spoke to the cook, whose name was Taste-that-which-is-good, to get supper ready for so many pilgrims. This done, he comes up again, saying, Come, my good friends, you are welcome here, and I am glad that I have a house to lodge you in; and while supper is being made ready, if you please, let us entertain one another with some good talk: so they all said, Agreed.

#speech[#smallcaps[Gaius]: Then Gaius said, Whose wife is this elderly lady? And whose daughter is this young woman?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: This woman is the wife of a man called Christian, a pilgrim of former times; and these are his four children. The young woman is an acquaintance of hers, whom she has persuaded to come with her on pilgrimage. The boys take after their father in everything, and long to tread in his steps; indeed, if they only see any place where the old pilgrim has lain, or any print of his foot, it brings joy to their hearts, and they long to lie or tread in the same place.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Gaius]: Then Gaius said, Is this Christian’s wife, and are these Christian’s children? I knew your husband’s father, and his father’s father too. Many of this stock have been good; their ancestors lived first at Antioch. Acts 11:26. Christian’s forefathers (I suppose you have heard your husband talk of them) were very worthy men. They have, more than any I know, shown themselves men of great virtue and courage for the Lord of the pilgrims, his ways, and those who loved him. I have heard of many of your husband’s relatives who have stood every trial for the sake of the truth. Stephen, who was one of the first of the family from which your husband sprang, was knocked on the head with stones. Acts 7:59, 60. James, another of this family, was killed with the edge of the sword. Acts 12:2. To say nothing of Paul and Peter, men of old of the family your husband came from, there was Ignatius, who was thrown to the lions; Romanus, whose flesh was cut in pieces from his bones; and Polycarp, who played the man in the fire. There was the man who was hung up in a basket in the sun for the wasps to eat; and the one they put into a sack and threw into the sea to be drowned. It would be utterly impossible to count up all of that family who have suffered injuries and death for the love of a pilgrim’s life. And I cannot help being glad to see that your husband has left behind him four such boys as these. I hope they will uphold their father’s name, and tread in their father’s steps, and come to their father’s end.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Indeed, sir, they are promising lads: they seem to choose their father’s ways with all their hearts.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Gaius]: That is what I said. So Christian’s family is likely to go on spreading far and wide over the face of the ground, and to be numerous upon the face of the earth; let Christiana seek out some young women for her sons, to whom they may be betrothed, and so on, so that the name of their father and the house of his forefathers may never be forgotten in the world.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: It is a pity that his family should fall and die out.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Gaius]: It cannot fall, but it may be diminished; but let Christiana take my advice, and that is the way to keep it up. And, Christiana, the innkeeper said, I am glad to see you and your friend Mercy here together, a lovely pair. And if I may advise, take Mercy into a closer relationship with you: if she is willing, let her be given to Matthew, your eldest son. It is the way to preserve descendants on the earth. So this match was agreed, and in due time they were married: but more of that later.]

Gaius went on and said, I will now speak on behalf of women, to take away their reproach. For as death and the curse came into the world by a woman, Gen 3, so also did life and health: God sent forth his Son, born of a woman. Gal 4:4. Indeed, to show how much those who came after abhorred the act of their mother, the women of the Old Testament longed for children, in the hope that this or that woman might be the mother of the Savior of the world. I will say again that when the Savior had come, women rejoiced in him before either man or angel did. Luke 1:42–46. I do not read that any man ever gave Christ so much as one groat; but the women followed him, and ministered to him from their own means. Luke 8:2, 3. It was a woman who washed his feet with tears, Luke 7:37–50, and a woman who anointed his body at the burial. John 11:2; 12:3. It was women who wept when he was going to the cross, Luke 23:27, and women who followed him from the cross, Matt 27:55, 56; Luke 23:55, and sat opposite his tomb when he was buried. Matt 27:61. It was women who were first with him on the morning of his resurrection, Luke 24:1, and women who first brought word to his disciples that he had risen from the dead. Luke 24:22, 23. Women therefore are highly favored, and show by these things that they are sharers with us in the grace of life.

Now the cook sent up word that supper was almost ready, and sent someone to lay the cloth and the plates, and to set out the salt and bread.

Then Matthew said, The sight of this cloth, and of this forerunner of the supper, awakens in me a greater appetite for my food than I had before.

#speech[#smallcaps[Gaius]: So let all the doctrines ministered to you in this life awaken in you a greater desire to sit at the supper of the great King in his kingdom; for all preaching, books, and ordinances here are only like the laying of the plates and the setting of salt on the table, compared with the feast that our Lord will make for us when we come to his house.]

So supper came up. And first a heave-shoulder and a wave-breast were set on the table before them, to show that they must begin their meal with prayer and praise to God. With the heave-shoulder David lifted up his heart to God; and with the wave-breast, where his heart lay, he used to lean on his harp when he played. Lev 7:32–34; 10:14, 15; Ps 25:1; Heb 13:15. These two dishes were very fresh and good, and they all ate heartily of them.

The next thing they brought up was a bottle of wine, as red as blood. Deut 32:14; Judg 9:13; John 15:5. So Gaius said to them, Drink freely; this is the true juice of the vine, which gladdens the heart of God and man. So they drank and were merry.

The next was a dish of milk with bread crumbled well into it; Gaius said, Let the boys have that, so that they may grow by it. 1 Pet 2:1, 2.

Then in due course they brought up a dish of butter and honey. Then Gaius said, Eat freely of this, for it is good to cheer and strengthen your judgments and understandings. This was our Lord’s dish when he was a child: “Butter and honey He shall eat, that He may know to refuse the evil and choose the good.” Isa 7:15.

Then they brought them a dish of apples, and they were very good-tasting fruit. Then Matthew said, May we eat apples, since it was with such fruit that the serpent beguiled our first mother?

Then Gaius said,

#quote[“Apples were they with which we were beguil’d, \
Yet sin, not apples, hath our souls defil’d: \
Apples forbid, if ate, corrupt the blood; \
To eat such, when commanded, does us good: \
Drink of his flagons then, thou church, his dove, \
And eat his apples, who art sick of love.”]

Then Matthew said, I raised that concern because a while ago I was sick from eating fruit.

#speech[#smallcaps[Gaius]: Forbidden fruit will make you sick; but not what our Lord has permitted.]

While they were talking like this, they were served another dish, and it was a dish of nuts. Song 6:11. Then some at the table said, Nuts spoil tender teeth, especially children’s teeth: and when Gaius heard this, he said,

#quote[“Hard texts are nuts, (I will not call them cheaters,) \
Whose shells do keep the kernel from the eaters: \
Open the shells, and you shall have the meat; \
They here are brought for you to crack and eat.”]

Then they were very merry, and sat at the table a long time, talking of many things. Then the old gentleman said, My good landlord, while we are cracking your nuts, if you please, solve this riddle:

#quote[“A man there was, though some did count him mad, \
The more he cast away, the more he had.”]

Then they all listened closely, wondering what good Gaius would say; so he sat still a while, and then replied like this:

#quote[“He who bestows his goods upon the poor, \
Shall have as much again, and ten times more.”]

Then Joseph said, I must say, sir, I did not think you could have worked it out.

Oh, Gaius said, I have been trained in this way a great while: nothing teaches like experience. I have learned from my Lord to be kind, and have found by experience that I have gained by it. There is one who scatters, and yet increases; and there is one who withholds more than is right, but it tends to poverty: there is one who makes himself rich, yet has nothing; there is one who makes himself poor, yet has great riches. Prov 11:24; 13:7.

Then Samuel whispered to Christiana, his mother, and said, Mother, this is a very good man’s house: let us stay here a good while, and let my brother Matthew be married here to Mercy before we go any further. Gaius the host overheard this and said, With all my heart, my child.

So they stayed there more than a month, and Mercy was given to Matthew as his wife.

While they stayed here, Mercy, as her custom was, kept making coats and garments to give to the poor, and by this she won a very good name for the pilgrims.

But to return to our story: after supper the lads wanted a bed, for they were weary with traveling. Then Gaius called for someone to show them their room; but Mercy said, I will put them to bed. So she put them to bed, and they slept well; but the rest sat up all night; for Gaius and they were such congenial company that they did not know how to part. After much talk about their Lord, themselves, and their journey, old Mr. Honest, the one who had put the riddle to Gaius, began to nod. Then Great-Heart said, What, sir, you are getting drowsy; come, rouse yourself, here is a riddle for you. Then Mr. Honest said, Let us hear it. Then Mr. Great-heart replied,

#quote[“He that would kill, must first be overcome: \
Who live abroad would, first must die at home.”]

Ha, said Mr. Honest, it is a hard one; hard to explain, and harder to practice. But come, landlord, he said, if you please, I will leave my part to you: you explain it, and I will hear what you say.

No, said Gaius, it was put to you, and you are expected to answer it. Then the old gentleman said,

#quote[“He first by grace must conquered be, \
That sin would mortify; \
Who that he lives would convince me, \
Unto himself must die.”]

That is right, said Gaius; good doctrine and experience teach this. For, first, until grace shows itself and overcomes the soul with its glory, the soul has no heart at all to oppose sin. Besides, if sin is Satan’s cords, by which the soul lies bound, how could it resist before it is freed from that weakness? Secondly, no one who knows either reason or grace will believe that a man who is a slave to his own corruptions can be a living monument of grace. And now that it comes to mind, I will tell you a story worth hearing. There were two men who went on pilgrimage; one began when he was young, the other when he was old. The young man had strong corruptions to grapple with; the old man’s were weak with the decay of nature. The young man kept his steps as even as the old one did, and was in every way as light as he. Now which of them had his graces shining the clearest, since both seemed to be alike?

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: The young man’s, without doubt. For what makes headway against the greatest opposition gives the best proof that it is strongest; especially when it also keeps pace with what meets with not half so much, as old age certainly does not. Besides, I have observed that old men have flattered themselves with this mistake: they take the decay of nature for a gracious conquest over corruptions, and so they have been apt to deceive themselves. Indeed, old men who are gracious are best able to advise those who are young, because they have seen the most of the emptiness of things; but still, when an old man and a young man set out together, the young one has the advantage of the clearest showing of a work of grace within him, though the old man’s corruptions are naturally the weakest. So they sat talking till daybreak.]

Now, when the household was up, Christiana told her son James to read a chapter; so he read the 53rd of Isaiah. When he had finished, Mr. Honest asked why it was said that the Savior was to come “out of dry ground,” and also that “He had no form or comeliness.”

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Then Mr. Great-Heart said, To the first I answer: because the church of the Jews, from which Christ came, had by then lost almost all the sap and spirit of religion. To the second I say: the words are spoken in the voice of unbelievers, who, because they lack the eye that can see into our Prince’s heart, judge him by the lowliness of his outside; just like those who, not knowing that precious stones are covered with a homely crust, when they have found one, because they do not know what they have found, throw it away again, as men do a common stone.]

Well, Gaius said, now that you are here, and since, as I know, Mr. Great-Heart is good with his weapons, if you please, after we have refreshed ourselves, we will walk out into the fields to see if we can do any good. About a mile from here there is a giant called Slay-good, who causes much trouble on the King’s highway in these parts; and I know where his haunt is. He is master of a band of thieves: it would be good if we could rid these parts of him. So they agreed and went: Mr. Great-Heart with his sword, helmet, and shield; and the rest with spears and staffs.

When they came to the place where he was, they found him with a man called Feeble-mind in his hand, whom his servants had brought to him, having caught him on the way. Now the giant was robbing him, intending afterward to pick his bones; for he was by nature a flesh-eater.

Well, as soon as he saw Mr. Great-Heart and his friends at the mouth of his cave with their weapons, he demanded to know what they wanted.

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: We want you; for we have come to avenge the wrongs of the many pilgrims you have killed when you dragged them out of the King’s highway: so come out of your cave. So he armed himself and came out, and they went into battle and fought for over an hour, and then stood still to catch their breath.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Slay-Good]: Then the giant said, Why are you here on my ground?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: To avenge the blood of pilgrims, as I told you before. So they went at it again, and the giant made Mr. Great-Heart give ground; but he came on again, and in the greatness of his spirit he let fly with such force at the giant’s head and sides that he made him let his weapon fall out of his hand. So he struck him, and killed him, and cut off his head, and brought it away to the inn. He also took Feeble-mind the pilgrim and brought him along to his lodgings. When they had come home, they showed the head to the family, and set it up, as they had done with others before, as a terror to any who might try to do as the giant had done in time to come.]

Then they asked Mr. Feeble-Mind how he had fallen into the giant’s hands.

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Feeble-Mind]: Then the poor man said, I am a sickly man, as you see; and because death used to knock at my door once a day, I thought I would never be well at home. So I took up a pilgrim’s life, and have traveled here from the town of Uncertain, where my father and I were born. I am a man with no strength at all, either of body or of mind; but I would, if I could, spend my life in the pilgrim’s way, though I can only crawl. When I came to the gate at the head of the way, the Lord of that place welcomed me freely; he made no objection to my sickly looks or to my feeble mind, but gave me what I needed for my journey, and told me to hope to the end. When I came to the house of the Interpreter, I received much kindness there; and because the hill of Difficulty was judged too hard for me, one of his servants carried me up it. Indeed, I have had much help from pilgrims, though none were willing to go as slowly as I am forced to go; yet whenever they came up with me, they told me to take heart, and said that it was the will of their Lord that comfort should be given to the feeble-minded, 1 Thess 5:14; and then they went on at their own pace. When I had got as far as Assault-lane, this giant met me there and told me to get ready for a fight. But, alas, feeble one that I was, I had more need of a restorative; so he came up and took me. I imagined he would not kill me. Also, when he had got me into his den, since I had not gone with him willingly, I believed I would come out alive again; for I have heard that no pilgrim who is taken captive by violent hands, if he keeps his heart whole toward his Master, is, by the laws of providence, to die by the hand of the enemy. I expected to be robbed, and robbed I certainly am; but, as you see, I have escaped with my life, for which I thank my King as the author and you as the means. I expect other blows as well; but this I have resolved on: to run when I can, to walk when I cannot run, and to creep when I cannot walk. As for the main thing, I thank him who loved me, I am settled; my way is before me, my mind is beyond the river that has no bridge, though, as you see, I have only a feeble mind.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: Then old Mr. Honest said, Were you not acquainted, some time ago, with a Mr. Fearing, a pilgrim?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Feeble-Mind]: Acquainted with him! Yes. He came from the town of Stupidity, which lies four degrees north of the city of Destruction, and as many away from where I was born; yet we knew each other well, for indeed he was my uncle, my father’s brother. He and I have been much of the same disposition; he was a little shorter than I, but even so we were much alike in make.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: I can see you knew him, and I am inclined to believe also that you were related to each other; for you have his pale look, a cast like his in your eye, and your speech is much alike.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Feeble-Mind]: Most who have known us both have said so; and besides, what I have read in him I have for the most part found in myself.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Gaius]: Come, sir, said good Gaius, take heart; you are welcome to me and to my house. Whatever you want, ask for it freely; and whatever you would like my servants to do for you, they will do it willingly.]

Then Mr. Feeble-mind said, This is an unexpected favor, like the sun shining out of a very dark cloud. Did giant Slay-good mean me this favor when he stopped me and resolved to let me go no further? Did he mean that after he had rifled my pockets I should go to Gaius my host? Yet so it is.

Now, just as Mr. Feeble-mind and Gaius were talking like this, someone came running and called at the door, and said that about a mile and a half away a pilgrim named Mr. Not-right had been struck dead on the spot by a thunderbolt.

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Feeble-Mind]: Alas! said Mr. Feeble-mind, has he been killed? He overtook me some days before I got this far, and wanted to keep me company. He was also with me when Slay-good the giant took me, but he was quick on his feet, and escaped; but it seems he escaped to die, and I was taken to live.]

#quote[“What one would think doth seek to slay outright, \
Ofttimes delivers from the saddest plight. \
That very Providence whose face is death, \
Doth ofttimes to the lowly life bequeath. \
I taken was, he did escape and flee; \
Hands cross’d gave death to him and life to me.”]

Now about this time Matthew and Mercy were married; Gaius also gave his daughter Phebe to James, Matthew’s brother, as his wife; after which they stayed about ten days longer at Gaius’ house, spending their time and the seasons as pilgrims usually do.

When they were about to leave, Gaius made them a feast, and they ate and drank and were merry. Now the hour had come when they must be gone; so Mr. Great-heart asked for the bill. But Gaius told him that at his house it was not the custom for pilgrims to pay for their lodging. He boarded them by the year, but expected his pay from the good Samaritan, who had promised him that at his return he would faithfully repay him whatever expense he had been put to for them. Luke 10:34, 35. Then Mr. Great-heart said to him,

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Beloved, you are faithful in what you are doing for the brothers, and for strangers, who have testified to the church about your love. You will do well to send them on their way in a manner worthy of God. 3 John 5, 6. Then Gaius said farewell to them all and to his children, and especially to Mr. Feeble-mind. He also gave him something to drink on the way.]

Now Mr. Feeble-mind, as they were going out of the door, acted as if he meant to stay behind. When Mr. Great-Heart noticed this, he said, Come, Mr. Feeble-mind, please come along with us; I will be your conductor, and you will fare as the rest do.

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Feeble-Mind]: Alas! I lack a suitable companion. You are all hearty and strong, but I, as you see, am weak; so I would rather come behind, so that my many infirmities do not make me a burden both to myself and to you. I am, as I said, a man of a weak and feeble mind, and I will be made to stumble and made weak by what others can bear. I will not like laughing; I will not like showy clothes; I will not like unprofitable questions. Indeed, I am so weak a man as to stumble at what others are free to do. I do not yet know all the truth; I am a very ignorant Christian man. Sometimes, if I hear others rejoice in the Lord, it troubles me because I cannot do so too. It is with me as it is with a weak man among the strong, or with a sick man among the healthy, or as with a lamp despised; so that I do not know what to do. “He who is ready to slip with his feet is like a lamp despised in the thought of him who is at ease.” Job 12:5.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: But, brother, said Mr. Great-Heart, it is part of my commission to comfort the feeble-minded and to support the weak. You must come along with us; we will wait for you; we will lend you our help; we will deny ourselves some things, both of opinion and of practice, for your sake; we will not get into doubtful disputes in front of you; we will be made all things to you, rather than have you left behind. 1 Thess 5:14; Rom 14; 1 Cor 8:9–13; 9:22.]

Now all this while they were at Gaius’ door; and just then, as they were in the heat of their talk, Mr. Ready-to-halt came by with his crutches in his hand, and he too was going on pilgrimage.

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Feeble-Mind]: Then Mr. Feeble-mind said to him, Man, how did you get here? I was just now complaining that I had no suitable companion, but you are just what I wished for. Welcome, welcome, good Mr. Ready-to-halt; I hope you and I may be some help to each other.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Ready-to-Halt]: I will be glad of your company, said the other; and, good Mr. Feeble-mind, rather than part, since we have had the good fortune to meet, I will lend you one of my crutches.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Feeble-Mind]: No, he said, though I thank you for your good will, I have no wish to limp before I am lame. Still, I think that when the occasion comes, it may help me against a dog.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Ready-to-Halt]: If either I or my crutches can do you a kindness, we are both at your service, good Mr. Feeble-mind.]

So they went on. Mr. Great-Heart and Mr. Honest went in front, Christiana and her children went next, and Mr. Feeble-mind came behind, and Mr. Ready-to-halt with his crutches. Then Mr. Honest said,

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: Please, sir, now that we are on the road, tell us some edifying things about some who have gone on pilgrimage before us.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Gladly. I suppose you have heard how Christian, in his day, met Apollyon in the Valley of Humiliation, and also what hard work he had to get through the Valley of the Shadow of Death. I think, too, you must have heard how Faithful was hard pressed by Madam Wanton, by Adam the First, by one called Discontent, and by Shame: four villains as deceitful as a man can meet on the road.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: Yes, I have heard of all this; but indeed good Faithful was hardest pressed by Shame: Shame never tired.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Yes; for, as the pilgrim rightly said, he of all men had the wrong name.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: But please, sir, where was it that Christian and Faithful met Talkative? He was another notable one.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: He was a confident fool; yet many follow his ways.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: He nearly deceived Faithful.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Yes, but Christian quickly showed Faithful a way to find him out.]

So they went on until they came to the place where Evangelist met Christian and Faithful and prophesied what would happen to them at Vanity Fair. Then their guide said, It was about here that Christian and Faithful met Evangelist, who prophesied to them the troubles they would meet with at Vanity Fair.

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: Is that so? I dare say it was a hard chapter that he read to them then.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: It was, but he gave them encouragement along with it. But why are we talking about them? They were a pair of lion-like men; they had set their faces like a flint. Don’t you remember how undaunted they were when they stood before the judge?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: Well, Faithful suffered bravely.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: So he did, and things just as splendid came of it; for Hopeful and some others, as the story tells, were converted by his death.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: Well, but please go on; for you know these things well.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Of all that Christian met with after he had passed through Vanity Fair, a man called By-ends was the chief of them.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: By-ends! What sort of man was he?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: A very crafty fellow, a downright hypocrite; one who would be religious whichever way the world went, but so cunning that he made sure never to lose or suffer for it. He had his own form of religion for every new occasion, and his wife was as good at it as he was. He would turn from opinion to opinion; yes, and argue for doing so, too. But as far as I could learn, he came to a bad end with his by-ends; nor did I ever hear that any of his children were ever thought well of by anyone who truly feared God.]

Now by this time they had come within sight of the town of Vanity, where Vanity Fair is held. So when they saw how near the town they were, they discussed with one another how they should pass through it; and some said one thing, and some another. At last Mr. Great-Heart said, As you may know, I have often been a conductor of pilgrims through this town. Now, I know a Mr. Mnason, Acts 21:16, a Cypriot by birth, an old disciple, at whose house we may lodge. If you agree, we will turn in there.

Agreed, said old Honest; Agreed, said Christiana; Agreed, said Mr. Feeble-mind; and so they all said. Now you must understand that it was evening by the time they got to the outskirts of the town; but Mr. Great-Heart knew the way to the old man’s house. So they came there; and he called at the door, and the old man inside knew his voice as soon as he heard it; so he opened the door, and they all came in. Then Mnason, their host, said, How far have you#super[+] come today? They said, From the house of Gaius our friend. I tell you#super[+], he said, you#super[+] have come a good long way. You#super[+] may well be tired; sit down. So they sat down.

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Then their guide said, Come, good sirs, how are you feeling now? I am sure you are welcome to my friend.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Mnason]: I welcome you too, said Mr. Mnason; and whatever you need, just say so, and we will do what we can to get it for you.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: Our great need, a while ago, was shelter and good company, and now I hope we have both.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Mnason]: As for shelter, you see what it is; but as for good company, that will be seen when it is put to the test.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Well, said Mr. Great-Heart, will you take the pilgrims up to their rooms?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Mnason]: I will, said Mr. Mnason. So he took each of them to his own room; and he also showed them a very fine dining room, where they might be together and eat supper until it was time to go to bed.]

Now when they were settled in their places, and a little cheered after their journey, Mr. Honest asked his landlord if there were many good people in the town.

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Mnason]: We have a few; for indeed they are only a few compared with those on the other side.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: But how can we manage to see some of them? For the sight of good men, to those who are going on pilgrimage, is like the moon and stars appearing to those who are sailing on the seas.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Mnason]: Then Mr. Mnason stamped his foot, and his daughter Grace came up. So he said to her, Grace, go and tell my friends, Mr. Contrite, Mr. Holy-man, Mr. Love-saints, Mr. Dare-not-lie, and Mr. Penitent, that I have a friend or two at my house who would like to see them this evening. So Grace went to call them, and they came; and after greeting one another, they sat down together at the table.]

Then Mr. Mnason their landlord said, My neighbors, as you#super[+] see, I have a company of strangers come to my house; they are pilgrims; they come from far away, and are going to Mount Zion. But who, he said, do you#super[+] think this is? pointing his finger at Christiana. It is Christiana, the wife of Christian, the famous pilgrim who, with Faithful his brother, was so shamefully treated in our town. At that they were astonished, and said, We little thought we would see Christiana when Grace came to call us; so this is a very heartening surprise. They then asked how she was, and if these young men were her husband’s sons. And when she had told them they were, they said, May the King whom you#super[+] love and serve make you#super[+] like your#super[+] father, and bring you#super[+] where he is in peace.

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: Then Mr. Honest (when they had all sat down) asked Mr. Contrite and the rest how things stood in their town at present.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Contrite]: You may be sure we are all hurry and bustle at fair-time. It’s hard to keep our hearts and spirits in good order when we are so encumbered. He who lives in such a place as this, and has to deal with such people as we have, needs a reminder to warn him to take care every moment of the day.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: But how peaceable are your neighbors now?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Contrite]: They are much more moderate now than they used to be. You know how Christian and Faithful were treated in our town; but lately, I say, they have been far more moderate. I think the blood of Faithful lies on them like a load to this day; for since they burned him, they have been ashamed to burn any more. In those days we were afraid to walk the street; but now we can show our faces. Then the name of a professing Christian was hated; now, especially in some parts of our town (for you know our town is large), religion is thought honorable. Then Mr. Contrite said to them, Tell me, how are you getting on in your pilgrimage? How does the country treat you?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: It goes with us as it goes with men on a journey: sometimes our way is clean, sometimes foul; sometimes uphill, sometimes downhill; we are seldom sure of anything. The wind is not always at our backs, nor is everyone we meet on the way a friend. We have met with some notable obstacles already, and what are still ahead we do not know; but for the most part we find true what was said long ago, A good man must suffer trouble.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Contrite]: You talk of obstacles; what obstacles have you met with?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: No, ask Mr. Great-Heart, our guide; he can give the best account of that.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: We have been attacked three or four times already. First, Christiana and her children were attacked by two ruffians, who they feared would take their lives. We were attacked by Giant Bloody-man, Giant Maul, and Giant Slay-good. Indeed, we attacked the last rather than he attacked us. And this is how it was: after we had been some time at the house of Gaius, my host and the host of the whole church, we decided one day to take our weapons with us and go and see if we could come upon any of those who are enemies to pilgrims; for we had heard that there was a notable one in those parts. Now Gaius knew his haunt better than I did, because he lived around there. So we looked and looked, until at last we spotted the mouth of his cave; then we were glad, and plucked up our spirits. So we went up to his den; and look, when we got there, he had dragged this poor man, Mr. Feeble-mind, into his net by sheer force, and was about to put an end to him. But when he saw us, supposing, as we thought, that he had another prey, he left the poor man in his hole and came out. So we fell to it hard, and he laid about him with all his strength; but in the end he was brought down to the ground, and his head was cut off and set up by the wayside as a terror to any who should practice such ungodliness afterward. That I am telling you the truth, here is the man himself to confirm it, who was like a lamb taken out of the mouth of the lion.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Feeble-Mind]: Then Mr. Feeble-mind said, I found this true, to my cost and to my comfort: to my cost, when he threatened every moment to pick my bones; and to my comfort, when I saw Mr. Great-Heart and his friends come so near with their weapons to deliver me.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Holy-Man]: Then Mr. Holy-man said, There are two things that those who go on pilgrimage need to have: courage, and a spotless life. If they do not have courage, they can never keep on their way; and if their lives are loose, they will make the very name of a pilgrim stink.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Love-Saints]: Then Mr. Love-saints said, I hope this warning is not needed among you; but truly there are many on the road who show themselves strangers to pilgrimage rather than strangers and pilgrims on the earth.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Dare-Not-Lie]: Then Mr. Dare-not-lie said, It’s true. They have neither the pilgrim’s clothing nor the pilgrim’s courage; they do not walk uprightly, but all awry with their feet; one shoe turns in, the other out; and their stockings are worn through at the back: here a rag, and there a tear, to the discredit of their Lord.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Penitent]: These things, said Mr. Penitent, they ought to be troubled about; nor are the pilgrims likely to have the honor they desire put upon them and their Pilgrim’s Progress until the way is cleared of such spots and blemishes. So they sat talking and passing the time until supper was put on the table; they went to it and refreshed their weary bodies, and then they went to bed.]

Now they stayed in the fair a long while, at the house of Mr. Mnason, who in the course of time gave his daughter Grace to Samuel, Christian’s son, as his wife, and his daughter Martha to Joseph.

The time they stayed here, as I said, was long, for it was not now as in former times. So the pilgrims got to know many of the good people of the town, and did them what service they could. Mercy, as she always did, worked hard for the poor; so their bellies and backs blessed her, and she was an ornament to her profession of faith there. And to tell the truth about Grace, Phebe, and Martha, they were all of a very good nature, and each did much good in her own place. They were also all of them very fruitful; so that Christian’s name, as was said before, was likely to live on in the world.

While they were staying here, a monster came out of the woods and killed many of the people of the town. It would also carry away their children and teach them to suck its whelps. Now, no man in the town dared so much as face this monster; but all fled when they heard the noise of his coming.

The monster was like no single beast on the earth. Its body was like a dragon, and it had seven heads and ten horns. It made great havoc among children, and yet it was ruled by a woman. Rev 17:3. This monster offered terms to men; and the men who loved their lives more than their souls accepted those terms. So they came under its rule.

Now Mr. Great-Heart, together with those who came to visit the pilgrims at Mr. Mnason’s house, made a covenant to go and fight this beast, in the hope that they might deliver the people of this town from the paws and mouth of so devouring a serpent.

Then Mr. Great-Heart, Mr. Contrite, Mr. Holy-man, Mr. Dare-not-lie, and Mr. Penitent went out with their weapons to meet him. Now at first the monster was very fierce, and looked on these enemies with great scorn; but they beat him so soundly, being sturdy men at arms, that they made him retreat; so they came home to Mr. Mnason’s house again.

The monster, you must know, had certain seasons when he came out and made his attempts on the children of the people of the town. At these seasons these valiant champions watched for him and attacked him again and again, so much so that in the course of time he became not only wounded but lame. Also, he has not made such havoc among the townsmen’s children as he did before; and some firmly believe that this beast will die of his wounds.

This, then, made Mr. Great-Heart and his fellows very famous in this town; so that many of the people who did not share their taste for these things still had a reverent esteem and respect for them. It was on this account, then, that these pilgrims did not come to much harm here. True, there were some of the baser sort, who could see no more than a mole, nor understand any more than a beast; these had no reverence for these men, and took no notice of their valor and adventures.
